.TH GXWI-PRINCIPALS-MANAGER 1 2026-10-19 "Peios gxwi-principals-manager 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
gxwi-principals-manager \- see and manage this machine's users and groups on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-principals-manager
.SH DESCRIPTION
Principals Manager shows the users and groups this machine knows, and lets an administrator manage the local ones.
A local principal is one the machine holds itself, in lpsd's store; a built-in one, such as SYSTEM or Administrators, is defined by authd and is the same on every Peios machine.
It is started from the launcher, and takes no arguments.
.PP
Users and Groups, in the bar, switch between the two lists, and Find narrows either.
Picking one shows it in full beside the list: for a user, their IDs, home, shell, SID, the ways they can sign in, their groups and their claims; for a group, its description, IDs, SID and members.
An administrator also sees what a local user signs in with and their SSH keys.
.PP
An administrator can make, edit, rename, disable, enable, and delete local users and groups; set a user's password, what they sign in with, and the ways they can sign in; add and remove their SSH keys and claims; and put users in groups and take them out, including built-in groups whose members are recorded here, such as Administrators.
Each change is a request to lpsd, as the person, and a refusal is shown in lpsd's words.
The machine always keeps an enabled administrator who can sign in, and a change that would leave none is refused.
Changes to how a user signs in apply from their next sign-in.
.PP
Privileges lists this machine's local policy: one record per user or group, under the registry key authd reads at every sign-in.
Each record can give privileges, an integrity level, an owner, and a default DACL.
A user's page says what they would get in all if they signed in now.
Records are made, changed and deleted in the registry directly, as the person.
.PP
Anyone can see the users and groups.
Changing them needs BUILTIN\eAdministrators, enabled in the person's token; without it, the window says You can look, but not change anything, and why.
The privileges records are changed by whoever the policy key's permissions let, which as shipped is Administrators.
.PP
The lists are read again every few seconds, so a change made with
.BR lps (1),
.BR reg (1)
or by another administrator appears by itself; Refresh (F5) reads everything at once.
.PP
It needs a desktop: run with no GXWI_DISPLAY, it says so and ends.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Sign-in policy records are stored under Machine\eGeneric\eAuthn\ePolicy and are consumed by authd when creating a new token.
.SH SECURITY
Local administration requires enabled Administrators membership as enforced by lpsd.
Local sign-in policy changes use registry write access.
The backend rejects changes that would remove the last enabled administrator able to sign in.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The application session ended normally.
.TP
.B 1
A connection, initialization, or application error prevented normal operation.
.TP
.B 64
Invalid command arguments.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GXWI_DISPLAY
The display connection supplied by the desktop session.
Launch through the desktop so inherited session descriptors and identity are available.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-principals-manager.toml
Its application declaration, which lists it in the launcher.
.TP
.I /run/ident.sock
authd's identity socket, where the users, groups and their details are read.
.TP
.I /run/lpsd/admin.sock
lpsd's admin socket, through which the local store is changed.
.TP
.B Machine\eGeneric\eAuthn\ePolicy
The registry key holding the privileges records.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lps (1),
.BR lpsd (8),
.BR authd (8).
.PP
Principals Manager, at learn.peios.org.
